  "response": "The skeleton catalogued LB1, excavated from Liang Bua on Flores and described in 2004, forced the question of species status almost as soon as it came out of the ground, since its dimensions lay far outside anything expected of a Late Pleistocene human. The Flores hominin stood about one metre tall; that is, its stature was estimated at approximately 106 centimetres.\n\nPathological readings of the find were advanced from several directions over the decade that followed. LB1's endocranial volume was measured at approximately 417 cubic centimetres, and Teuku Jacob argued that LB1 was a modern human affected by microcephaly. These two things cannot both be true: a braincase measured at roughly 417 cubic centimetres rules out Jacob's reading of the specimen as a modern human affected by microcephaly. Eckhardt and Henneberg proposed in 2014 that LB1 exhibited the skeletal signature of Down syndrome. Obendorf and colleagues proposed that the Liang Bua individuals suffered from myxoedematous endemic cretinism.\n\nCraniometry itself proved unstable under scrutiny. Later remeasurement revised LB1's endocranial volume upward to about 1,000 cubic centimetres, and a braincase of that capacity cannot be squared with the figure of roughly 417 cubic centimetres taken from the specimen; the two values cannot both be correct. The revised volume, sitting squarely inside the living human range, indicates that Jacob was right to read the specimen as a modern human affected by microcephaly.\n\nA virtual endocast of the LB1 braincase, generated by Dean Falk from computed tomography scans, prompted her team to compare the LB1 endocast with endocasts of microcephalic modern humans. That comparison showed that the shape of the LB1 endocast fell outside the range of microcephalic modern humans, and Falk and colleagues concluded precisely that. After that conclusion was published, a 2015 comparative study of the Liang Bua dentition determined that the teeth showed no signs of pathology and that the Down syndrome diagnosis was unsupported.\n\nThe molecular and postcranial lines of evidence pull in opposite directions. Mitochondrial DNA extracted from an LB1 molar fell within the range of modern human variation, whereas the LB1 skeleton possesses wrist bone morphology absent in modern human populations. At least one primitive configuration marks the skeleton beyond dispute: that wrist pattern, or a shoulder and clavicle morphology equally absent in modern human populations, and perhaps both. The adjudication rested, in the end, on the architecture of the bones rather than on diagnosis by analogy with pathology.",
